{"id": "live-343", "title": "BFT Rental, LLC v. City of Fayetteville (Eminent Domain Compensation Appeal)", "category": "eminent-domain", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-10-28", "jurisdiction": "Arkansas Court of Appeals, Division III (No. CV-25-506); Washington County Circuit Court (No. 72CV-23-3442)", "state": "AR", "amount": "$112,180 just-compensation deposit, affirmed as the ceiling on recovery", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://opinions.arcourts.gov/ark/courtofappeals/en/524709/1/document.do", "summary": "The Arkansas Court of Appeals affirmed a directed verdict in favor of the City of Fayetteville after BFT Rental, LLC failed to appear for the jury trial set to determine just compensation for a condemned easement, leaving BFT's recovery capped at the City's $112,180 pre-trial deposit. The court held BFT forfeited appellate review of its claim that the circuit court erred in allowing its attorney to withdraw while denying a continuance, because BFT raised that objection for the first time in a post-trial motion rather than contemporaneously.", "significance": "Confirms that a condemnee's right to a jury's independent assessment of just compensation can be lost through default just like any other civil claim, and that a statutory quick-take deposit can become the final ceiling on recovery once the condemnee fails to put on evidence of greater value at trial. Commercial property owners facing eminent domain actions should treat scheduling disputes and counsel transitions as issues requiring an immediate, on-the-record objection rather than a basis to raise after an adverse trial outcome.", "judge": "N. Mark Klappenbach", "tags": ["eminent domain", "condemnation", "just compensation", "arkansas", "directed verdict"]}
